    "answer": "Now I have a thorough understanding of the codebase. Let me lay out the analysis and options.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"The defect in `RunReviewPairAsync` (RelayDriver.ReviewPair.cs) is that BOTH stage_done events are published and BOTH status entries are updated at the pair's barrier \u2014 after both stages finish \u2014 rather than per-stage. The root causes are: (1) `RunStageAsync` returns the `Stopwatch` still running, so it accumulates sibling-wait time, and (2) the caller calls `RecordPairStageAsync` \u2192 `RecordStageAsync` only after both awaits complete, meaning the fast sibling's stopwatch is stopped late (after waiting for the slow sibling) and its stage_done/status update is delayed to the barrier. The UI then shows misleading durations and 'Running' status for the already-finished sibling.\\n\\nThe fix requires:\\n1. **Stop the stopwatch in RunStageAsync** (not in RecordStageAsync), capturing elapsed in `StageRunResult`.\\n2. **Record each stage immediately** when its task completes, not at the barrier. This means calling `RecordPairStageAsync` for stage 7 right after `await reviewTask`, and for stage 8 right after `await visualTask`. For the fast-visual case (visual finishes before review awaits it), check `visualTask.IsCompleted` before recording review, so visual's stage_done fires with its own ~46s timing.\\n3. **Barrier unchanged** \u2014 `RunReviewPairAsync` still returns only after both tasks resolve, so stage 9 waits for both.\\n4. **Failure paths unchanged** \u2014 red checks \u2192 FlagAsync still aborts the pair.\\n5. **Downstream consumers** (ApplyStageEventMetric, drain summary, status.json) already handle stage_done events one at a time and don't assume ordering.\\n\\nKey files to modify:\\n- `src/VisualRelay.Core/Execution/RelayDriver.ReviewPair.cs` \u2014 restructure recording in `RunReviewPairAsync`, stop stopwatch in `RunStageAsync`\\n- `src/VisualRelay.Core/Execution/RelayDriver.Invocation.cs` \u2014 `RecordStageAsync` no longer needs to stop the stopwatch (already stopped)\\n- `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/RelayDriverReviewPairTests.Orchestration.cs` \u2014 add new tests\\n\\nThe test suite (`test.sh`) must remain green with new tests covering: fast-visual/slow-review, slow-visual/fast-review, barrier regression, event-order tolerance.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"**Option A \u2014 Sequential recording (review first, then visual)**: After `await reviewTask`, immediately call `RecordPairStageAsync` for review. Then after `await visualTask`, immediately record visual. Stop the stopwatch in `RunStageAsync`. Simplest change \u2014 only the review stage gets per-stage timing when visual is the slower one. Does NOT handle fast-visual (visual finishes before review) \u2014 visual would still inherit its stage_done delay. Risk: low (minimal restructuring); gap: fast-visual case not fixed.\",\n    \"**Option B \u2014 Completion-order recording with IsCompleted check**: Await reviewTask. Before recording review, check `visualTask?.IsCompleted == true` and if so, process visual first (record its stage_done early). Then record review. Then await visual if still running. Stop the stopwatch in `RunStageAsync`. Handles both fast-review and fast-visual orderings. Risk: low-to-moderate (slightly more restructuring, duplicate cost-accrual lines, but no new abstractions). Best balance of correctness and minimality.\",\n    \"**Option C \u2014 Task.WhenAny completion loop**: Build a dictionary of `{stageNum \u2192 Task<StageRunResult>}`, loop with `Task.WhenAny`, process each completion immediately. Fully general completion-order handling. Risk: moderate (larger restructure, more surface area for bugs in the loop/branching for triage-skip/red-flag paths). Highest correctness guarantee but also highest change surface.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
